# M24.2 -- run RTEMS's own `hello` sample (testsuites/samples/hello) on Renode's mainline
# Zynq UltraScale+ Cortex-R5 platform (docs/open-questions.md question 144), on rpu0 only
# (lock-step BSP; apu*/rpu1 left halted, same convention as the M24.1 spike's setup.resc and
# this build's own bundled scripts/single-node/zynqmp_zephyr.resc, which runs a different RTOS
# on the same rpu0/zynqmp.repl combination). UART0 output is captured to a file via
# `CreateFileBackend` (headless-safe; `showAnalyzer` needs a GUI window and is not used here).
#
# `hello` explicitly disables the clock driver (see testsuites/samples/hello/init.c:
# CONFIGURE_APPLICATION_DOES_NOT_NEED_CLOCK_DRIVER) and only prints three lines once, so a
# short virtual run (2s) is ample -- expected output stated in REPORT.md before running:
#   *** HELLO WORLD TEST ***
#   Hello World
#   *** END OF HELLO WORLD TEST ***

:name: zynqmp-rpu-lockstep-hello

using sysbus
using sysbus.cluster0
using sysbus.cluster1

mach create "zynqmp-rpu-hello"

machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/cpus/zynqmp.repl

uart0 CreateFileBackend @$ORIGIN/hello_uart.log true

macro reset
"""
    cluster0 ForEach IsHalted true
    cluster1 ForEach IsHalted true
    rpu0 IsHalted false

    sysbus LoadELF $ORIGIN/hello.exe cpu=rpu0
"""

runMacro $reset

start
emulation RunFor "2"
quit
